        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<br />
Problems associated with space motion sickness and clear vision during space flight have no realistic solution given existing countermeasures. Preflight adaptation, while it appears to be effective, requires substantial investments in crew time, and crewmembers must experience some level of motion sickness if the preflight adaptation environment is a serious analog of flight. Inflight centrifugation may be the ultimate vestibular countermeasure, however, rapid implementation is impracticable, and like preflight adaptation protocols, crewmembers will experience motion sickness on both ground and flight centrifuges.</p>
<p>The purpose of this study is to investigate both the prevention of motion sickness and retinal slip through the use of stroboscopic vision. These investigations are intended to develop countermeasures to help crewmembers avoid disorientation, meet physical requirements for emergencies, treat motion sickness without side effects, and assure safe control of the spacecraft.</p>
<p>Specific Aims:<br />
<li>To study the effects of stroboscopic light on the motion sickness induced by performing visual tasks while wearing reversing prisms.</li>
<li>Research, develop, and build a set of goggles that will use polymer dispersed liquid crystal or ferroelectric liquid crystal display material as electronic shutters.</li>
<li>To study the effects of stroboscopic light on the motion sickness induced by using the goggles developed as a part of specific aim 2.</li>
<li>To evaluate the ability of stroboscopic vision due to liquid crystal goggles to prevent motion sickness in a range of experimental conditions, including parabolic flight and a slow rotation room.
<p>APPROACH:<br />
The research for this proposal is divided into four phases. The Phase I protocol requires a three-part research plan that will: (1) verify the effectiveness of stroboscopic vision in preventing motion sickness induced with left-right visual reversing prisms; (2) investigate the effect of flash rate and duration on prism- induced motion sickness; and (3) study the adaptation of motion sickness susceptibility under stroboscopic illumination.</p>
<p>In Phase II, stroboscopic goggles using LCD electronic shutters will be constructed to replace the stroboscopic flash generator for use in environments where ordinary stroboscopic flash is not feasible.</p>
<p>In Phase III the effect of these goggles will be verified; then tested in unique provocative environments; including both parabolic flight and tests within the Navy's Slow Rotation Room centrifuge.</p>
<p>Finally, in Phase IV the probable mechanisms involved with stroboscopic presentation and the prevention of motion sickness and retinal slip will be investigated using the newly constructed stroboscopic goggles. These investigations are directly responsive to the goals of NSBRI's neurovestibular research program: To develop countermeasures that help crewmembers: avoid disorientation, meet the physical requirements for emergencies, treat motion sickness without side effects, and assure safe control of the spacecraft.</p>
<p>RESULTS:<br />
Results from this study are not available.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
